VALIDITY, ARITHMETIC AND SHARED EFFECTS.

This document supplies the named rules cited by each individual entry in
"Every primary opcode.english" and by inventory.tsv. A rule citation is part
of the entry's meaning. It does not replace the entry's English operation.
No parser or executable English grammar is defined here.

FRAME, TYPES AND PAIRS.

All named register words must exist in the current frame. Every source must
have been assigned a type-compatible value along every incoming path.
Every required pair must consist of two adjacent existing words with the
proper long or double type. Its first index need not be even. A four-bit
index limits the pair start, not necessarily its second member to four bits.
Overlapping operands use the original source values before writes.
Verification merges type state at joins and invalidates a former pair half
when an overlapping narrow write destroys its partner. These checks do not
grant arithmetic conversion between int bits and references.

RESULT, HANDLER AND RETURN.

The result instruction must immediately follow its producing invoke in
the instruction array AND in actual normal control flow. Its category must
match the return prototype. An object result from filled-new-array also
qualifies. The result may instead be ignored; it cannot be picked up later.
No branch may enter a result instruction. No exception path produces the
normal result of an invocation that did not complete normally.

move-exception may appear only at the first instruction of a handler.
The caught object may be ignored by omitting that instruction. It is not a
load of a globally last-thrown exception. The handler's type is determined
by its protected ranges and caught types, not by an arbitrary incoming value.

Return category must match the method prototype: void, single-word primitive,
wide primitive, or reference. Reference assignment compatibility, narrow
primitive requirements, initialized-object state and constructor obligations
still apply. A return does not run the next instruction in this frame.

EXIT.

Method exit includes the runtime's method-level synchronization obligations.
When a synchronized method has acquired its implicit monitor, normal or
exceptional method exit must release it as required. Explicit monitor-enter
operations must follow legal monitor discipline; a return instruction is not
a substitute for explicit monitor-exit cleanup. Invalid lock state may be
rejected by verification or cause IllegalMonitorStateException under runtime
monitor checks. Do not infer a successful return from an invalid method.
ART compatibility/instrumentation paths can have additional exceptional
behavior; those are not portable new opcode semantics.

BRANCH AND SWITCH.

Offsets are signed code-unit counts relative to the branch instruction.
Targets must be real executable instruction starts in this same method,
not a second unit, a data payload, a move-result or a move-exception entry
that lacks its required context. goto, goto/16 and every if forbid zero
offset; goto/32 permits it. A conditional instruction does not write flags.
Equality accepts compatible integer values or object references as allowed
by verification. Reference comparisons test identity. Ordered if tests use
signed integers; a float needs a separate comparison instruction first.

Each switch has the matching aligned payload kind and a fully bounded table.
Sparse keys strictly increase. Packed keys must not wrap their signed range.
Each selected case target is relative to the switch, not the payload.
A missing case continues by three units. Payload layout and overflow-safe
extent calculation are in "Instruction formats and data payloads.english".

COUNT-LIST AND COUNT-RANGE.

A compact list contains zero through five WORDS. Select the first count
register fields in C,D,E,F,G order. A wide parameter supplies two successive
argument words whose register indices are consecutive. A range contains
zero through 255 consecutive words beginning at its encoded start. The
entire interval must exist without wrapping an index calculation.
The method's outgoing-word capacity must accommodate calls. The count and
word kinds must agree with the selected prototype. An instance receiver
uses one word; a static invocation does not invent one.

INVOCATION.

Method indices must resolve to an accessible member of the right kind.
Virtual methods are neither static nor private nor constructors. Direct
instance calls target the permitted private method or instance initializer.
Constructors require the appropriate uninitialized instance. Other uses
must not consume an uninitialized reference as an ordinary initialized one.
Calls to names beginning with '<' are forbidden except the permitted
invoke-direct invocation of <init>; <clinit> is not explicitly called here.
Prototype arguments and return kind must agree. Protected access includes
the receiver restrictions imposed by the caller's class context.

Before DEX 037, invoke-super and invoke-static cannot use interface method
references. From 037, interface-super selection follows the most specific
non-overridden interface implementation and static interface methods can
be referenced. This changes contextual validity of existing opcodes,
not the size or number of their primary opcode bytes.

Resolution/kind/access failures include NoSuchMethodError, IllegalAccessError,
IncompatibleClassChangeError and class-resolution failures as applicable.
A missing concrete implementation can cause AbstractMethodError. Excessive
call depth may cause StackOverflowError. A null instance receiver causes
NullPointerException. A native method can fail linkage with UnsatisfiedLinkError.
The invoked method, including a framework or native method, can throw other
exceptions or have arbitrary effects according to its own contract. This
catalogue does not mistake those contracts for a finite DEX opcode exception
list. Signature-polymorphic and custom calls add their individually stated
MethodHandle and bootstrap contracts.

ARRAY AND ARRAY-CREATION.

Array references, integer indices and element categories must match the
opcode: int/float word, long/double pair, object, boolean, byte, char, short.
Read a byte or short with sign extension; read a char with zero extension.
Read a Boolean as zero or one. Narrow stores retain the field's or element's
width; a verified Boolean input already obeys Boolean value constraints.
An object store must be compatible with the array's ACTUAL component class;
static reference compatibility alone does not eliminate ArrayStoreException.
Array stores do not initialize the component class merely by storing a value.

An array index must be at least zero and smaller than its actual length.
Null checks and bounds checks are execution conditions, not permissions
granted by a valid 23x encoding. A failed access does not supply a normal
load result or successfully replace the target element.

new-array designates a valid array type, with at most 255 dimensions. It
allocates only the requested outer array, not a recursively populated
multidimensional structure. Primitive elements start at zero; reference
elements start null. Negative lengths and allocation failure are distinct.
filled-new-array and its range form cannot take wide long/double components.
The inspected ART implementation additionally restricts supported primitive
filled components to int, while the public opcode table states the broader
single-word rule. The audit records that restriction explicitly; it is not
silently promoted to the portable table's definition. ARRAY-CREATION is thus
source-qualified for the disputed narrow/float primitive forms.

FIELD.

The reference identifies a field by its class, name and declared type, not
by a byte address. Instance operations require an instance field and a
compatible initialized receiver. Constructor verification allows the narrow
explicit exception for assigning this class's own non-inherited instance
fields before the required super/this constructor call. This does not grant
general use of the uninitialized object. Static operations require a static
field and initialize the class that actually DECLARES the resolved field.
The class named in the original symbolic reference need not declare an
inherited field. Opcodes must match the field category and access rules.

Final-field stores are limited by the defining class/initializer rules;
they are not generally writable because the reference index encodes them.
Narrow reads sign-extend byte and short, zero-extend char, and represent
Boolean as zero or one. Word and wide forms preserve int/float and
long/double bits under matching declared types. Reference stores preserve
identity and must be assignment-compatible with the declared field type.
Field errors include NoSuchFieldError, IllegalAccessError and
IncompatibleClassChangeError for wrong static/instance kind, plus RESOLUTION.
Some such errors may be caught during verification/resolution rather than
by a particular runtime check. Malformed typing is not promised to become
a particular Java exception at the point of the field instruction.

Volatile is a FIELD property, not a separate portable iget-volatile opcode.
A volatile field access must supply the applicable Java memory-model
ordering and atomicity. Ordinary accesses do not acquire monitor ordering
merely because they use the same instruction family. A wide register pair
does not itself prove atomicity of an ordinary shared long/double field.
ART's read/write barriers and object representation are implementations.

RESOLUTION, NEW AND INITIALIZATION.

Valid indices and descriptors are necessary but not sufficient for successful
resolution. Resolving a missing class can raise NoClassDefFoundError; access
or linkage can raise the appropriate IllegalAccessError or LinkageError
subclass. Member-resolution errors are stated by FIELD and INVOCATION.
An implementation may discover failures at a permitted earlier stage;
do not require every invalid method to start and then throw at the opcode.

new-instance must name a non-array class. Interfaces and abstract classes
cannot be instantiated. The public static constraints disallow such new
targets, while the runtime also contains InstantiationError checks. This
is a distinction between method validity and runtime defensive checking.

INITIALIZATION: initialize the resolved declaring class when required before
new-instance allocation, static field access, or static invocation. Follow
the language/runtime initialization order, including required superclasses
and applicable superinterfaces, and coordinate concurrent initialization.
Recursive initialization by the same thread does not simply run the same
initializer again. Initializer execution may have arbitrary effects.
A non-Error thrown by the initializer is normally wrapped in
ExceptionInInitializerError; an Error can propagate directly. A class left
erroneous causes NoClassDefFoundError on subsequent required initialization.
Allocation can fail with OutOfMemoryError, including allocation needed while
resolving constants. Resolution alone, const-class, and array creation do
not automatically run a component class initializer.

INTEGER ARITHMETIC.

Int is signed two's-complement thirty-two-bit and long sixty-four-bit.
Addition, subtraction, multiplication and negation keep their low width
bits and never throw for overflow. Division truncates toward zero.
Minimum signed integer divided by minus one returns that same minimum,
without an overflow exception. Its remainder is zero. A zero integer
divisor throws ArithmeticException. For other dividends the remainder has
the dividend's sign or is zero, and its magnitude is smaller than the
divisor's magnitude. For example minus seven divided by three is minus
two and its remainder is minus one, not positive two.

Shift counts are masked, not range-checked: five low bits for int, six for
long. A long shift's count occupies one INT register, not another pair.
Arithmetic right shifts sign-fill; logical right shifts zero-fill.
Left shifts discard outgoing high bits. Literals are sign-extended before
word arithmetic and bitwise operations. rsub subtracts the REGISTER from
the LITERAL, reversing the usual register-then-literal operand order.

FLOAT.

Float and double mean IEEE binary32 and binary64 values. Arithmetic rounds
to nearest with ties to an even significand and preserves gradual underflow,
except the expressly different conversion and remainder rules. Results
include signed zero, subnormal values, signed infinity and NaN. Numeric
overflow, underflow, invalid floating arithmetic and floating division by
zero do not raise integer ArithmeticException or a hardware trap promised
by DEX. Do not assert portable preservation of a NaN's payload or sign.

Addition of opposite infinities gives NaN. Subtraction of like infinities
gives NaN. Multiplying zero by infinity gives NaN. Dividing zero by zero
or infinity by infinity gives NaN. Finite nonzero divided by zero gives an
infinity with the quotient sign. Finite divided by infinity gives signed
zero; products and quotients use the exclusive-or of operand signs.
With nearest rounding, exact cancellation of opposite nonzero finite values
gives positive zero. Adding two negative zeros gives negative zero; the
usual signed-zero rules govern the other zero sums and differences.
A NaN input propagates NaN classification for floating arithmetic.

Floating remainder uses an INTEGER quotient truncated toward zero in the
mathematical definition, not the IEEE nearest-integer remainder operation.
Do not implement that definition by first rounding a floating quotient and
then multiplying it; that can overflow or round differently. A finite
dividend and infinite divisor return the dividend. A zero dividend with
nonzero finite or infinite divisor preserves its signed zero. An infinite
dividend, zero divisor, or NaN operand produces NaN. A nonzero finite
remainder has the dividend's sign.

Floating comparisons return integer minus one, zero or one. Both signed
zeros compare equal. A cmpl instruction chooses minus one on ANY NaN;
cmpg chooses one. Comparing NaNs does not itself throw.

Floating-to-integer conversion truncates toward zero; NaN becomes zero;
out-of-range values saturate at the signed target endpoints, including
infinities. Integer-to-float and narrowing double-to-float use nearest-even.
Converting an int to long or double is exact. Long-to-int discards high
bits. Float-to-double preserves finite values exactly; no bit-exact NaN
payload conversion is promised.

MONITOR AND COMMON EXCEPTION BOUNDARY.

Monitors are reentrant and owned by threads. Entry may block and establish
acquire ordering; final release establishes release ordering. Entry/exit
does not assert fairness, a timeout or an implicit call to Object.wait.
Null references fail; an unowned exit fails with IllegalMonitorStateException.
Verification additionally tracks admissible structured monitor use and
aliasing. A monitor's state is not stored in an ordinary virtual register.
The public table's special post-instruction exception attribution for
monitor-exit must survive a lowering even if the runtime's helper API
looks like an ordinary function call.

Each entry's exception column lists its portable operation-specific failures
and shared categories. It is not an exhaustive finite list of all exceptions
which arbitrary called code, initialization, resource exhaustion, platform
compatibility paths or asynchronous runtime events could cause. Rejection
of an ill-typed or malformed method belongs to validation, not to normal
execution of that method. No claim about error priority is made when the
sources do not establish which of several simultaneously failing checks
takes precedence. The audit preserves the known public-table discrepancies.

SOURCES.

B mathematical details and individual opcode notes; F operand encodings;
D field/method/prototype/code/handler definitions and version history;
C structural constraints. Java language memory, initialization and numeric
rules are corroborated by the source packet listed in fixtures/dex/sources.
ART implementation excerpts are explicitly implementation evidence, not
an undocumented replacement for the portable instruction contract.
